[sub_resource type="RectangleShape2D" id="RectangleShape2D_kill"]
size = Vector2(8000, 40)

; A short, narrow floor (400 px, well under Flatlands' 1200 or even Bowl's
; ~1040 including its walls) flanked on both sides by a nine-high column of
; #18's hazard zone, flush against the floor's own edge. There is no gap
; between "safe ground" and "the wall" and no gap between the floor and open
; air beyond it -- the hazard IS the wall. Reaching the stage's edge is the
; mistake; there is nothing past it to fall into, only something to touch.
;
; The column is built by placing nine Hazard.tscn instances stacked with no
; vertical gap (each is 48 px tall, so nine cover 432 px running from well
; above spawn height down to well below the floor's top surface) rather than
; by overriding Hazard's shape to one tall RectangleShape2D. Hazard.tscn's
; own comment offers both routes; stacking wins here because overriding a
; property on a node *inside* an already-instanced sub-scene only takes
; effect through Godot's editable-children mechanism (an `[editable
; path=...]` scene-file directive), which is easy to get subtly wrong by
; hand outside the editor and would parse clean without anything here to
; catch it being wrong. Stacking only places top-level instances the same
; way Gauntlet's hazard already does, so it carries no format risk.
;
; A single 1800 px/s horizontal shove from either spawn (x = +-120) covers
; the 80 px to the nearest wall's inner face (x = +-200) in about three
; physics ticks, well before gravity has pulled the body more than a couple
; of pixels off spawn height -- so the column's 432 px of coverage, centred
; on spawn height with generous margin both ways, is never actually probed
; at its own edges by the ring-out sweep, but is sized to hold even if a
; future weapon change adds vertical knockback to the shove.
;
; A KillZone still sits well below the floor, matching every other stage's
; shape, even though this stage's sealed wall-to-wall-hazard construction
; means nothing should ever reach it under the knockback the stage was
; designed for: it is a safety net against a body somehow launched clear
; over the top of a hazard column by a future high-arc knockback, not a
; second death rule -- it is the same KillZone.gd every other stage already
; uses, so reaching it still eliminates through the one path (US-6).

